WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:13.000
The rabbinites, the people who believe in judaism and practice judaism, the way we practice it ourselves, were only a minority.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Very important to remember, the, I mean if we want to call it an orthodox world at the time, was a minority to the kairites.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
In addition to this, the community says, sorry, Al-Ambar says, he has to pay me.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:43.000
So, judaism, and this is a Jewish community, for the first time had access to the law.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:57.000
What does this mean? It meant the following revolution. That the community, the lay people, were in charge of directing the affairs of the kairites.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Not the rabbis, not the clergy, before it was the clergy. Only the clergy had access to the Torah.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:13.000
And therefore the coming people, or the lay people, or the committee, or the gemayel, or the parnasim, had no access.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:26.000
So of course here comes the first revolution, Mishneh Torah. Through the Mishneh Torah, all of a sudden the people, the coming people, could organize themselves and could supervise the rabbis.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Another thing, which is very, very important, the commentary to the Mishneh. Somebody tells you the Talmud is this and that, a person with a little bit of knowledge, knowledge of Arabic also, because that was the common language, he could open and he could have access to the Talmudic law.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Now, the two things, community, for first time now the community, the power is in the hands of the community, and we have a book, and the term is of course Torah.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Besides this, Haramban, with his own personality, changed the habits of the community. Let me give you one example from Halab.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:24.000
There is a question, the community of Halab, they ask him, are we permitted to listen to the songs of the Goliath?

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
They love Arabic music. Are we permitted to go and listen to Arabic music?

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Haramban, whether you agree with him or not, he believed no.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:48.000
He said, no, you should not listen. If you want to listen, then you have to write your own words in Hebrew.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:03.000
This is the beginning of the institution that we call Pakashot, the whole notion of Pakashot, of Bismolim, that you take Arabic music and you put Arabic music into Hebrew words.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:10.000
That was a direct effect of Haramban's position.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Now, here comes one more point and then we are going to see what happened.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:27.000
In the East, in the Middle East, Jews were in commerce. They were actively in international commerce.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:34.000
The whole Mediterranean and the whole commerce with India was in the hands of the Jews in the Middle East.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Now, Haramban, not only that he promoted this type of leadership, but here comes something interesting.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000
For the first time, it is the businessmen who become the people of power in the Jewish community.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:59.000
It is not the physician, it is not the scientist, it is not the scholar, it is the man of action.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:06.000
A final note, Haramban, believe it or not, never occupied a rabbinic position in his life.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
You know what he was? He was the president of the community, not the rabbi of the community.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:24.000
And we come here, it changes the whole world of the lay community, the lay people, and above all, the possibility that the president who is the one who is acting.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:34.000
Now, when Haramban left Spain as a child, probably at the age of 12 or 13, Spain was the paradise.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Jews had wealth, it was the only country, the only country in which there was absolutely no inter-Semitism.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Jews were in power and it was a kind of paradise.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Now, all of a sudden, the book Mishneh Torah reaches the West.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:05.000
And as it reaches the West, people there, the clergy in the West, become a little bit uncomfortable.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:16.000
There is first criticism on the Mishneh Torah by a famous person by the name of the Ramal, from France, representing the Ashkenazi tradition.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:24.000
And it's a very virulent attack on the Mishneh Torah. We will see why in a second. Very virulent.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.000
And the attack is on two grounds. Interesting.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Number one is the attack says because my father does not bring the sources.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Say what is Haramban, but Haramban knew the sources. That was number one.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:47.000
As I write in one of my articles in English, I say, sources there are in the Mishneh Torah.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:53.000
But if one of them hears the trick, the only person who can make the Shakalaka, hold the Jewish tradition,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:00.000
is somebody who knows, you have to know the whole Talmud, Babylonian and Palestinian Talmud.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.000
You have to know the whole Yosefta, you have to know the Sifra and the Sifre.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Now, if you know this material, you read the Mishneh Torah, and immediately Haramban in every single archive is a key term.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.000
But if you know the thing, really if you know it, immediately you could tell where it comes from.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:31.000
So the sources are there, but what's more important is the following.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:37.000
That the Rabbi himself, when he writes Halakha, he does not write the sources.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Not only he didn't write the sources, it was not customary for the Rabbis to point out the sources. Why?

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Because either you're a Rabbi, then immediately you know where the sources are, or you should know where the sources are,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.000
or you're not a Rabbi, in which case the sources are not there.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:08.000
The second point, why Haramban was attacked, is because Haramban said that God has no shape, no form, no size,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.000
is not a body, and of whom? And of whom is that?

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:19.000
And if you believe that God has a body, then that is not Jewish belief.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:28.000
In France at the time, and in Ashkenazim, Germany, the common Jew believed that God has a body.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:41.000
And for them to see that their belief was heresy, that molested them, that bothered them very much.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:51.000
Now, because of this, a whole attack on Haramban and the mission of Torah began in France.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:08:02.000
From France, it passes to Spain, in a great Imam, we're going to see later on about him, Rabbi Meir Abul Ahadiyah.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:13.000
Rabbi Meir Abul Ahadiyah is the first Sephardic Jew from Spain to attack Haramban, the attack on Haramban.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Now, why does he attack Haramban? Again, repeating the same sources.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.000
By good luck, many years ago, I found a letter.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:34.000
I found a letter from the president of the community in Toledo, Ibn Shushan, referring explicitly to Rabbi Meir Abul Ahadiyah.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:43.000
The letter is very sad, and some of the words I would not dare say to anybody, of course not to a rabbi, but they were contemporized.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:48.000
And Shushan, Rabbi Shushan, who was the president of the community, he said like this,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.000
You are saying that now with the mission of Torah, people are not saying Talmud.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:59.000
Well, let me tell you, hundreds of times we begged you, please teach us Talmud.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.000
And you would refuse to teach us Talmud.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.000
Now, I tell you what you don't like. It's not that we started because we are not saying Talmud.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:15.000
What you don't like is the fact that before you gave arbitrary decisions and nobody was able to check on you.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.000
And now, we could check on your decisions.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000
It's a very bitter letter. It's a very polemical letter.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.000
But that letter, in a sense for me, it tells you the crux of the problem.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:34.000
The rabbis in the West had centuries in control of the community.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:43.000
With that book, the community, the control became limited. It was contained.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:49.000
I point out in my book that eventually Rabbi Meir Abraham was a very honest man.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.000
When he criticized Aram Bam, he was a young man.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:05.000
And he was misled.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:15.000
But later on, when he finds out that Aram Bam died, he writes a beautiful poem, apologizing, begging and saying ha-Aram Bam,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.000
and saying point of fact that he was fooled.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:27.000
But without knowledge or without knowing, he becomes something that spread throughout Spain.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:38.000
Many, many rabbis started attacking not only the Mishneh Torah, but also the person of Aram Bam.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:49.000
Something which helped the anti-mongolians was the Morene Bukhil, the Charter of the Perplexed.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Now, if you read the Charter of the Perplexed, if you have philosophical knowledge, if you are a philosopher,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.000
you realize, number one, that that is not a book of philosophy.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.000
It's not religious philosophy at all.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.000
What it is, it is a critical analysis.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:15.000
A deep thought of what Judaism is all about.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.000
But it's not philosophy.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.000
My mother, as you say, my mother's attitude was Aristotle.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:22.000
Yes?

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
Aristotle was a great Greek philosopher.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.000
Now, I pointed out this again.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.000
I'm very, very careful.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.000
I made many studies of it.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.000
I published this in Hebrew, in English, in French.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:32.000
Many studies.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:38.000
We are sure that Aram Bam consistently, in the major portions, disagrees with Aristotle.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.000
But he disagrees with Terech Erez.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.000
It is always Curtis, Aram Bam.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.000
He's always very careful.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.000
And when he disagrees with a philosopher, he doesn't go and rush on.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.000
But he points out what are the basis for the disagreement.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:08.000
Now, the people who attacked Aram Bam, they used the fetters of Morene Bukiri as a pretext,

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:15.000
saying that whatever, whoever is going to read this book is going to become perverted.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.000
It's going to go outside the level of Judaism.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.000
It's going to become assimilated.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.000
It's going to become a glory.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.000
And therefore, we should not read the Morene Bukiri.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:32.000
Now, not only we should not read Morene Bukiri, but by implication, not only we should not read it.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.000
And therefore, we deauthorize Aram Bam.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.000
Therefore, what is Mishneh Torah is also not good.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.000
And we should not rely on his decisions.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:42.000
And there was a whole movement.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:48.000
The movement actually was directed from France, from the north of France.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.000
And the Jews in Spain just followed that ideology.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.000
Now, some things happened.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:00.000
And I want to tell you one of the most interesting, interesting things that happened,

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.000
and what really this is.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:13.000
Two Sephardic boys, at the time they were young men, went to study at Yishivot in France.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.000
And the rabbi there was called Shlomo Minahar.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.000
Or Shlomo or Solomon from Montpellier.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.000
Minahar, yes?

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.000
And Montpellier.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.000
And he was arch-enemy of Aram Bam.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.000
Arch-enemy of Aram Bam.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:34.000
And he instigates two young rabbis.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.000
If you want me to give you the name, I'll give you the name, but...

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.000
You want me to give you the name?

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:46.000
But these two young rabbis, one of them became later on very famous,

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.000
they didn't follow him.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.000
Very well, one of them...

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.000
Goes to the Dominicans,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.000
which is a very...

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:04.000
It's a known order of monks, Dominicans.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.000
Very anti-Semitic.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:14.000
Till today, the Dominicans are very, very aggressively anti-Semitic.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.000
And then they go to the Franciscans,

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.000
the same rabbi goes to the Franciscans,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:24.000
and they say, listen, the Moreno will come,

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.000
not only against Jewish religion,

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.000
but against Christianity.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.000
Now, you go to the Dominicans,

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:36.000
you go to the Dominicans and the Franciscans,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.000
the guys are looking for this opportunity,

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.000
for a Jew,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.000
tells us, come to us,

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:49.000
asking us to test an opinion

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.000
as something pertaining to Judaism.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.000
What does this mean?

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.000
See, people do not understand the Christian Church.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.000
The Christian Church is an extraordinary...

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.000
One of the best organizations in the world.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.000
The surviving, I don't know what it is, 2,000 years.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.000
Extraordinary organization.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.000
And what they do, they are very punctilious.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.000
They don't do things haphazardly.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.000
What they said is,

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.000
here this establishes a precedent.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.000
What is the precedent?

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.000
You Jews,

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:21.000
recognize our authority

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.000
in matter of religions.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.000
Now, the Dominicans read the book,

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:31.000
Morea Dei Buchim,

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.000
helped by the Franciscans,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.000
and I want to give you the date,

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.000
the precise date.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
The precise date is...

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.000
The name of the governor was Calvario Maros,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.000
and he, this is burned when?

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.000
When?

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.000
When the highway bridges are blind?

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:59.000
It's burned in Montpellier,

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.000
exactly in the year 1232.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.000
December 1232,

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.000
Morea Dei Buchim,

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.000
hundreds of copies of Morea Dei Buchim,

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.000
are brought into the main square of Montpellier,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.000
in the presence of this rabbi,

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.000
and other rabbis who were,

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.000
and there is a public burning

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.000
of the Morea Dei Buchim.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.000
Now,

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.000
when this happened,

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:35.000
there were many rabbis who were,

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:37.000
up to the moment,

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.000
silently.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.000
And should I read for you

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.000
how the description of this event?

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Should I read or is it too painful?

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.000
What do you think?

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.000
Yes?

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.000
No, because it's painful.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Isn't it painful?

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
For me it's painful, I don't know.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.000
Let me...

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.000
I have to find eventually

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.000
the place where...

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.000
I have to read eventually.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.000
So, many rabbis

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.000
they were shocked.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000
One of the things that shocked the rabbis

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.000
is that a year, exactly a year after burning the Morea Dei Buchim,

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.000
came the Dominicans,

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:35.000
they had a very strong apparatus

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:37.000
for anti-Semitism.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.000
What they used to do?

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.000
They used to take yeshiva students,

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:43.000
used to make them convert,

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:46.000
you know, money, power, this and that.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.000
Used to make them study the Talmud.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.000
And he said, show us in the Talmud.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.000
What is in the Talmud?

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:54.000
There is something against going.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.000
Going, why they go in?

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.000
There they go if they don't see.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.000
A year exactly after,

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.000
carloads

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.000
of copies of the Talmud

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.000
are brought to Montpellier

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.000
and they are burned in the same square.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.000
When Jews protested

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:18.000
and they say,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.000
you have no right

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:23.000
to intervene in the internal affairs

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.000
of the community.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.000
The Dominicans and the Protestants said, we do.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.000
Because your rabbis

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:35.000
came to us exactly a year ago

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.000
and they asked us to look into the Morea Dei Buchim.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:42.000
By asking us to look into the Morea Dei Buchim,

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:45.000
it means that you recognize

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.000
our authority

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.000
in matters of religion.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.000
Now, what we did is,

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.000
not only will we look into the Morea Dei Buchim,

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:58.000
but we also will look into the Talmud.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Not only to burn the Talmud,

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:04.000
but one of the things which really was a catastrophe.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.000
They asked the king

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:10.000
and they obtained a permission

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.000
to have a predicatore,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.000
predicatore, a preacher,

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:16.000
from the church,

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:18.000
every Shabbat,

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:22.000
to preach in the Jewish synagogue

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.000
about Christianity

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.000
and the falsehood of the Talmud.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.000
From that moment on,

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.000
the whole atmosphere

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:35.000
in southern France,

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.000
northern Spain,

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:38.000
it changed.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.000
Specifically, what the worst thing that happened

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:42.000
from my point of view,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.000
I mean, I say it as a historian,

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:45.000
it's not, you know, of course,

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:47.000
the burning of the Talmud is a disaster,

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.000
but in the long term,

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:53.000
having a preacher inside the synagogue,

